WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
SIBO is one of the topics I'm asked
about the most and I'm glad I finally

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.680
have the chance to dig into the research
in this two-part video series.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.120
I'll be answering the following
questions: Is SIBO real?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.960
How reliable are the diagnostic tests?
Does a low FODMAP diet help?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.360
And if you have SIBO or IBS symptoms,
what should you do about it?

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.936
Let's go find out.

00:00:21.936 --> 00:00:29.269
"Tests, Fiber, and Low FODMAP for
Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth (SIBO)"

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.960
Gastrointestinal symptoms
like abdominal pain

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.240
and bloating account for millions
of doctor visits every year.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.800
One of the conditions that may be
considered for such a nonspecific

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.760
presentation of symptoms is SIBO:
Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.040
a concept that has gained
popularity on the internet,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.880
in addition to certain clinical
and research circles.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.680
SIBO is broadly defined
as the excessive growth of

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.920
bacteria in the small intestine, and
is typically treated with antibiotics.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.600
But dispensing antibiotics to patients

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.600
with such nonspecifc, common
symptoms is not without risks.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.520
You could be fostering
antibiotic resistance,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.040
there could be side effects,
and wiping out your good bacteria

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.240
could set you up for an invasion
of bad bugs like C. diff,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:22.560
all for a condition that
may not even be real.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.080
Even alternative medicine
journals admit that SIBO

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.120
is being overdiagnosed,
creating confusion and fear.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.200
SIBO testing is overused and
overly relied upon. Diagnoses are

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.160
often handed out quickly and
without adequate substantiation.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.200
Patients can be indoctrinated into
thinking SIBO is a chronic condition

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.200
that cannot be cleared and will
require lifelong management.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.280
This is simply not true for most and
is an example of the damage done

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.560
by overzealousness.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:59.840
The monster that we now perceive SIBO
to be may be no more than a phantom.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.080
The traditional method for a diagnosis
was a small bowel aspiration,

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.960
an invasive test where a long tube
is snaked down your throat

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.640
to take a sample and count
the bugs down there.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.480
But this method has been almost
entirely replaced with breath testing.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.960
See, normally, a sugar called glucose
is almost entirely absorbed

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.920
in the small intestine, so it never
makes it down to the colon.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.200
So if there is bacterial fermentation
of that sugar, that suggests

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.240
you have bacteria up in your
small intestine and can detect

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.440
that fermentation because the
bacteria produce specific gases

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.720
that get absorbed in your blood stream
and then are breathed out from

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.320
your lungs, which can then be detected
with a breathalyzer-type machine.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.320
Previously, a sugar called lactulose
was used, but lactulose breath tests

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.400
weren't found to reliably detect
the overgrowth of bacteria,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.400
so researchers switched to glucose.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.520
But when glucose was finally put
to the test, it didn't work either.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.000
The bacterial load in the
small intestine was similar

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.960
for those testing positive or negative,
so that's not a useful test either.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.560
It turns out glucose can make it
down to your colon after all.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.160
They labeled the glucose dose with
a tracer and found that almost half

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.640
of the positive results from glucose
breath tests were false positives

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.320
because they were just fermenting
it down in their colon

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.200
where your bacteria are
supposed to be.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.320
So patients incorrectly labeled with
SIBO may undergo multiple courses

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.760
of antibiotics for a problem
they don't even have.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.200
Why do experts keep recommending
breath testing then?

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.440
Oh, you mean the experts at
the conference supported by

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.640
a breath testing company, most of
whom had personally received funds

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.800
from SIBO testing
or antibiotic companies?

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.160
And even if we could properly
diagnose it, does it even matter?

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.360
In those with digestive symptoms,
there is a range of positivity for SIBO

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.000
from approximately 4% to 84%
—all over the map—

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.840
and there may be no difference in
symptom scores between those testing

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.120
positive versus those testing negative,
so testing positive could mean anything.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.600
Who cares if some people have bacteria
growing in their small intestines

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.600
if that doesn't necessarily
correlate with symptoms?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.080
Now antibiotics can make people
with irritable bowel-type symptoms

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
diagnosed with SIBO feel better, so
doesn't that prove SIBO was the cause?

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.680
No, because it can make just
as many people feel better

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.000
who are negative for SIBO.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.200
Currently rifaximin is most often used
for SIBO, but it's not FDA-approved

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.760
for SIBO, so the cost
can be prohibitive.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.720
In fact, no drug has
been approved for SIBO,

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.680
so even if you have good
insurance, you may be shelling

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.240
out-of-pocket as
much as $50 a day,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.240
and you typically take
it for two weeks.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.920
And while antibiotics may
help in the short-term,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.640
they may make things
worse in the long-term.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.160
Those who are given a course of
antibiotics are more than three times

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.840
as likely to report more bowel symptoms
four months later than controls.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.320
So what can we do for
these kinds of symptoms?

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.800
That's exactly what I'm
going to turn to next.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.240
If you test more than a thousand
patients suffering from

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.960
irritable bowel-type symptoms
such as excess gas, bloating,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.000
diarrhea, abdominal pain
for longer than six months,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.280
who don't appear to have anything
more serious going on, such as

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.440
inflammatory bowel disease, a
significant percentage were found

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:36.240
to be suffering from lactose intolerance:
intolerance to the milk sugar lactose.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.320
In infancy we have an enzyme
in our small intestines that

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:44.440
digests milk sugar, but most of us
understandably lose it after weaning.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.720
Although a genetic mutation has
led to the persistence of the enzyme

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.640
in a minority of adults, about
75% of the world's population

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.200
malabsorb lactose and have
lactose intolerance after age 30.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.040
A third, though, were
diagnosed with SIBO:

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.960
Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.040
The evidence for SIBO and IBS
symptoms is shrouded in controversy,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.080
predominantly because of the
fact that the breath tests used

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.640
in clinical practice to diagnose
SIBO are not valid,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.160
as I explored in the last video.
And it's not even clear

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.480
what the implications are of having
more versus less bacteria growing

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.600
in your small intestine since the number
don't seem to correlate with symptoms.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.440
It turns out it's not the
number, but the type.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.080
It's the kind of bugs you have
growing in your small intestine.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:40.720
So it's small intestinal microbial
dysbiosis, not overgrowth in general,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.880
but the wrong kind of growth
that appears to underlie

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:47.040
symptoms associated with functional
gastrointestinal disorders

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.680
like irritable bowel syndrome.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.880
How can you prevent
this from happening?

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.240
Well, the symptoms appeared
be correlated to a significant drop

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.960
in the number of Prevotella species.
Remember them?

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.600
Prevotella are healthy fiber feeders,
suggestive of a higher fiber intake

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.720
in healthy individuals, while the bugs
found more in symptomatic patients

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:13.920
ate sugar, which may reflect a
higher dietary intake of sugars.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.760
Yes, but correlation
doesn't mean causation.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.400
To prove cause and effect
you have to put it to the test,

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.800
which is exactly what they did.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.920
Switching a group of healthy individuals
who habitually ate a higher fiber diet

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.120
to a more typical standard American
low fiber diet with lots of sugar

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.240
produced striking results
within just seven days.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.280
First, 80% developed new
gastrointestinal symptoms

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.200
out of the blue, such as bloating
and abdominal pain that resolved

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:49.280
on resumption of their more healthful,
habitual higher fiber diet. And the

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.080
diet related changes in the small
intestinal microbiome were predictive

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.640
of symptoms and linked to an
alteration in intestinal permeability;

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:02.480
in other words, they developed
a leaky gut within seven days.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.840
And while some went from
SIBO positive to SIBO negative,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.800
or SIBO negative
to SIBO positive,

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.280
it didn't matter, since the
number of bacteria growing

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:15.240
didn't correlate with symptoms. It
was the type of bacteria growing.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:20.440
And no wonder their guts got leaky.
Short-chain fatty acid levels plummeted.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.520
Those are the magical by-products that
our good gut bugs make from fiber,

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.120
which play an important role
in intestinal barrier integrity,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:31.640
meaning keeping our gut
from getting leaky. So while

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.680
we don't have sound data that
something like a low FODMAP diet

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.160
has any benefit for
SIBO-symptom patients,

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.040
there have been more than a
dozen randomized controlled trials

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:45.800
putting fiber to the test, and overall
there was a significant improvement

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.560
in symptoms among those randomized
to increase their fiber intake.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:53.840
That may actually help explain
why high-fiber, plant-based diets

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.840
may prevent so many common
diseases — the effect such diets have

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.080
on the composition and metabolic
activity of our microbiome.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.440
Our good gut bugs take the
plant residues like fiber

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.720
and produce health-promoting and
cancer-suppressing metabolites

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.960
like those short-chain fatty acids
which have profound

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.360
anti-inflammatory properties.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.480
All the evidence points
to a physiological need

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.000
for about 50 grams of fiber a day,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.120
which is the amount contained
in the traditional African diet

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.560
and associated with the prevention
of westernized diseases.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:29.920
That's approximately twice
what's typically recommended

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.640
and three times more than what
most people are getting day-to-day.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.440
Perhaps it should be no
surprise we need so much.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.840
Even though we split from
chimpanzees millions of years ago,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.560
there's still broad congruency
in the composition

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.800
of our respective microbiomes
to this day.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.160
While they're still eating their 98-99%
plant-based diets to feed their friendly

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:59.825
flora with fiber, we've largely removed
fiber-rich foods from our food supply.

